% The two reified array builtins over a DECLARED array rather than an inline literal,
% which is a different route through lib/flatzinc/builder.ml: `array_bool_or([a, b], r)`
% builds its operand list from an array expression written at the call site, while this
% resolves an array-typed identifier and then an element of it. Both the three-element
% width and `xs[1]` as an operand are unexercised by the other bool_ models, which all
% use two-element inline lists.
%
% It also pins the array output line for Booleans alongside a genuine `var bool` array
% that the search has to assign -- test/models/bool_out_sat.fzn has one whose elements
% are folded constants, so the printer's array path has never been driven by a solved
% array until now.
%
% The run:
%
%   `~xs[1]' is a unit clause, so xs[1] is false at the root.
%     -> array_bool_and's FORWARD clause (~s \/ xs[1]) makes s false.
%   decide xs[2] = false  (indomain_min; xs[1] is already fixed, so xs[2] is first)
%     -> `xs[2] \/ xs[3]' makes xs[3] true
%     -> `xs[2] \/ ~xs[3]' is violated.  CONFLICT
%   so xs[2] = true
%     -> array_bool_or's BACKWARD clause (r \/ ~xs[2]) makes r true
%     -> xs[3] is free, indomain_min settles it false
%
% WHAT THIS MODEL PINS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Measured by deleting each decomposition's
% instances and re-running, not by reading the trace above:
%
%   array_bool_or's backward clause  -- PINNED. Delete it and this model fails: r is
%     never forced and the answer changes.
%   array_bool_and's forward clause  -- NOT pinned, even though the trace above uses it.
%     s comes out FALSE, which is also what indomain_min would settle on for a variable
%     nothing constrains, so deleting the clause leaves the printed answer identical.
%     That direction is pinned by test/models/bool_and_sat.fzn instead, where s's
%     counterpart must come out true. Said here rather than left implied, because a
%     trace line in a comment is not evidence that a model can see the line go missing.
%
% Ground truth, with no reference to the search: xs[1] is false; xs[2] \/ xs[3] and
% xs[2] \/ ~xs[3] together entail xs[2]; r = or(xs) is then true and s = and(xs) is
% false because xs[1] is not. Unique except for xs[3], which the default strategy
% settles at false. r = true and xs[2] = true are both the opposite of the indomain_min
% default.
array [1..3] of var bool: xs :: output_array([1..3]);
var bool: r :: output_var;
var bool: s :: output_var;
constraint array_bool_or(xs, r);
constraint array_bool_and(xs, s);
constraint bool_clause([], [xs[1]]);
constraint bool_clause([xs[2], xs[3]], []);
constraint bool_clause([xs[2]], [xs[3]]);
solve satisfy;
